Desert plants have special features in their leaves that reduce the loss of water, which is scarce in the desert (any two):
- a **thick cuticle on the leaf surfaces**, which cuts down water loss from the surface;
- **sunken stomata**, with the stomata arranged in deep pits, so that water loss by **transpiration** is minimised;
- **leaves reduced to spines**, as in Opuntia, so that there is hardly any leaf surface to lose water from; the photosynthetic function is then taken over by the flattened stems.

(a) The diagram shows a **simple stirred-tank bioreactor**: a cylindrical vessel with a **curved base** that holds the **culture broth**. Labels (any four are expected):
- **Motor**, mounted on top, which turns a central shaft;
- **Flat bladed impeller**, the stirrer blades on the shaft that mix the broth;
- **Foam breaker**, near the top of the shaft, which breaks up the foam;
- **Sterile air**, bubbled in at the base through a sparger to supply oxygen;
- **Acid/base for pH control**, an inlet used to keep the pH optimum;
- **Steam for sterilisation**, an inlet used to sterilise the vessel;
- **Culture broth**, the medium in which the cells grow.

(b) A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a **curved base to facilitate the mixing of the reactor contents**. The stirrer can then keep the whole culture evenly mixed, with no dead corners where cells and medium could settle, so oxygen and nutrients reach the cells throughout the reactor.
